<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hilary of Poitiers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 310–c. 367</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Hilarius Pictaviensis, Saint Hilary, Hammer of the Arians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hilary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> was a 4th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Poitiers and a Doctor of the Church, called the Hammer of the Arians for his opposition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a>. Born at Poitiers to distinguished pagan parents, he turned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity">Nicene Christianity</a> through study of the Scriptures. Banished to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> after the synod of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beziers">Béziers</a> in 356, he spent nearly four years in exile, writing De synodis and De trinitate, the first successful expression in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> of the Nicene theology worked out in Greek. Back in his diocese in 361, he opposed the homoion creed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and encouraged Martin, the future bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, to found a monastery at Ligugé; according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> he died in Poitiers in 367.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hilary-of-poitiers.jpg" alt="Hilary of Poitiers" width="330" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard de Montbaston et collaborateurs. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hilaryofpoitiers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
